  "topics": "Inversion, topics, emphasis and ellipsis according to communicative purpose",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "Topic and contrast: a crucial difference"
      },
      "Putting an element at the start of the sentence can do two different things, and the pronoun shows which one.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Type",
            "Example",
            "Function"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Topic, with a pronoun",
              "Il libro l'{pp:ho letto}.",
              "As for the book…"
            ],
            [
              "Contrastive focus, no pronoun",
              "IL LIBRO {pp:ho letto}, non il giornale.",
              "That's what I read, not something else."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "The first says what we are talking about. The second corrects an expectation, and is marked in speech by stress on the fronted element.",
      {
        "h": "Verb first, subject last"
      },
      "With verbs of appearing, arriving and happening, a subject that brings new information naturally follows the verb: *{pp:È arrivata} una lettera. {pp:È successo} un guaio. Manca il sale.* Putting the subject first would suggest that we were already talking about it.",
      {
        "h": "Fronting for style"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Device",
            "Example",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Fronted complement",
              "Di lui, nessuno {pr:seppe} più nulla.",
              "gives the topic prominence"
            ],
            [
              "Fronted adjective",
              "Grande {pr:fu} la sorpresa.",
              "elevated, literary"
            ],
            [
              "Hanging topic",
              "La politica, non ne {ip:voglio} sapere.",
              "lively, spoken"
            ],
            [
              "Rhetorical inversion",
              "Bella, la vita!",
              "exclamatory"
            ],
            [
              "Quanto a",
              "Quanto a me, {ip:resto}.",
              "introduces a new topic"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "What can be left out"
      },
      "Ellipsis means dropping what the context supplies. It's normal in conversation, and in some written genres it's the rule.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Context",
            "Example",
            "Omitted"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Reply",
              "— {ip:Vieni}? — Io no.",
              "the verb"
            ],
            [
              "Headline",
              "Maltempo al Nord, tre feriti.",
              "the verb"
            ],
            [
              "Notice",
              "Vietato fumare.",
              "{ip:è}"
            ],
            [
              "Literary nominal style",
              "Silenzio. Una finestra accesa. Nessuno.",
              "everything but the essential"
            ],
            [
              "Coordination",
              "Lui {ip:ama} il mare; lei, la montagna.",
              "the repeated verb"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "In the last example the comma stands in for the missing verb. It's one of the few cases where a comma can separate a subject from what follows."
    ]
  },
